PART 2 — OPTIMIZE ⚡
What is OPTIMIZE?
"OPTIMIZE is a command that compacts many small parquet files into fewer large parquet files — improving query performance and reducing storage overhead"
Simple Analogy 📚
Imagine your bookshelf:
1000 tiny booklets scattered everywhere → Hard to find anything, takes long time
OPTIMIZE = Combine all tiny booklets into 10 thick organized books
Same content, much easier and faster to find!
Why Small Files Are a Problem?
How small files accumulate:

Streaming pipeline (writes every 1 minute):
→ Minute 1:  file_001.parquet (5MB)
→ Minute 2:  file_002.parquet (5MB)
→ Minute 3:  file_003.parquet (5MB)
...
→ After 1 day:  1440 files × 5MB = 7.2GB
→ After 1 week: 10,080 files × 5MB = 50GB
→ After 1 month: 43,200 files!!! 😱
Why are Small Files Slow?
Query: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE status = 'delivered'

With 43,200 small files:
→ Spark opens file_001 → reads → closes
→ Spark opens file_002 → reads → closes
→ Spark opens file_003 → reads → closes
...43,200 times!

Each file open/close = overhead
→ More time on file management than actual reading!
→ S3 charges per API call = expensive!
→ Query that should take 10 seconds takes 10 MINUTES!
How OPTIMIZE Works
BEFORE OPTIMIZE:
part-001.parquet   5MB  ──┐
part-002.parquet   5MB    │
part-003.parquet   5MB    │
part-004.parquet   5MB    ├──→ OPTIMIZE combines these
part-005.parquet   5MB    │
...                       │
part-200.parquet   5MB  ──┘
Total: 200 files × 5MB = 1GB

AFTER OPTIMIZE:
part-new-001.parquet  512MB ──┐ Same 1GB data
part-new-002.parquet  488MB ──┘ but only 2 files!

Result:
→ Same data ✅
→ Fewer files ✅
→ Queries 10x faster ✅
→ Less S3 API calls ✅
→ Less cost ✅
OPTIMIZE Syntax
-- Basic OPTIMIZE
OPTIMIZE employees;

-- OPTIMIZE specific partition only
OPTIMIZE employees WHERE date = '2024-03-14';

-- OPTIMIZE with ZORDER (most powerful!)
OPTIMIZE employees ZORDER BY (department);

-- OPTIMIZE with multiple ZORDER columns
OPTIMIZE employees ZORDER BY (department, city);

**********************************************************************

ZORDER — What is it?
Zorder means nothing grouping all similar data
"ZORDER physically sorts and co-locates related data together in same parquet files so queries can skip irrelevant files completely"
WITHOUT ZORDER:
file_001.parquet → Hyderabad, Bangalore, Chennai, Mumbai (mixed!)
file_002.parquet → Hyderabad, Chennai, Mumbai, Pune (mixed!)
file_003.parquet → Bangalore, Hyderabad, Pune, Chennai (mixed!)

Query: WHERE city = 'Hyderabad'
→ Must read ALL 3 files to find Hyderabad records
→ Reads unnecessary data!

WITH ZORDER BY (city):
file_001.parquet → Hyderabad, Hyderabad, Hyderabad (grouped!)
file_002.parquet → Bangalore, Bangalore, Chennai (grouped!)
file_003.parquet → Mumbai, Mumbai, Pune (grouped!)

Query: WHERE city = 'Hyderabad'
→ Delta knows Hyderabad is ONLY in file_001!
→ Skips file_002 and file_003 completely!
→ Data Skipping = Much FASTER! ⚡
ZORDER Real Example
-- Common query pattern in your company:
SELECT * FROM sales 
WHERE region = 'South' AND product = 'Mobile';

-- ZORDER by frequently filtered columns:
OPTIMIZE sales ZORDER BY (region, product);

Before ZORDER:
Query reads: 500 parquet files (100GB) → 5 minutes

After ZORDER:
Query reads: 12 parquet files (2.4GB) → 8 seconds!

Same data — 37x faster! 🚀
When to Use OPTIMIZE?
Use OPTIMIZE when:
✅ Queries are getting slower over time
✅ Table has streaming writes (creates many small files)
✅ After bulk INSERT operations
✅ After loading large CSV/JSON data
✅ When DESCRIBE DETAIL shows too many files

How frequently?
→ Streaming tables     → Run daily (many small files daily)
→ Batch pipeline tables → Run weekly
→ Rarely updated tables → Run monthly

Best time to run:
→ Off-peak hours (midnight/early morning)
→ OPTIMIZE is resource intensive!
→ Runs as a Spark job on your cluster
OPTIMIZE — What to ZORDER By?

GOLDEN RULE:
ZORDER by columns that appear most in WHERE clauses!

Example queries in your company:
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 123;
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE date = '2024-03-14';
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'pending';

Best ZORDER:
OPTIMIZE orders ZORDER BY (customer_id, date, status);

DON'T ZORDER by:
❌ Columns with very high cardinality (unique IDs with billions of values)
❌ Columns never used in WHERE clause
❌ More than 4 columns (diminishing returns)

