3️⃣ MERGE INTO vs INSERT OVERWRITE
INSERT OVERWRITE
What: Completely replaces existing table or partition data with new data

-- Overwrite entire table
INSERT OVERWRITE sales.orders
SELECT * FROM new_orders;

-- Overwrite specific partition only
INSERT OVERWRITE sales.orders
PARTITION (year=2024, month=3)
SELECT * FROM new_orders
WHERE year=2024 AND month=3;

Key Points:
Deletes existing data first — then inserts new
Entire table or partition replaced
Fast — simple operation
No row level comparison
Good for full refresh scenarios


MERGE INTO
What: Upsert operation — INSERT new records, UPDATE existing records, DELETE removed records — all in one command
MERGE INTO target t
USING source s ON t.order_id = s.order_id

WHEN MATCHED AND s.status = 'cancelled' 
  THEN DELETE

WHEN MATCHED 
  THEN UPDATE SET t.revenue = s.revenue, 
                  t.status = s.status

WHEN NOT MATCHED 
  THEN INSERT (order_id, revenue, status)
  VALUES (s.order_id, s.revenue, s.status);


Key Points:
Row level precision — updates only changed rows
Handles insert, update, delete in single operation
Slower than INSERT OVERWRITE — row comparison needed
Best for CDC (Change Data Capture) scenarios
SCD Type 1 and Type 2 implementations use MERGE

