Managed Table — Where Does Data Get Stored? 🎯
Answer Depends on TWO Things
1. Are you using Unity Catalog or Hive Metastore?
2. Did you set Storage Location or not?
Scenario 1 — Hive Metastore (Old Way)
Default location:
dbfs:/user/hive/warehouse/database_name.db/table_name

Example:
CREATE TABLE sales.orders ...

Stored at:
dbfs:/user/hive/warehouse/sales.db/orders/
DBFS (Databricks File System):
DBFS is NOT real storage!
DBFS is a virtual layer that maps to
your cloud storage behind the scenes

AWS   → S3 bucket Databricks created
Azure → ADLS Databricks created
GCP   → GCS Databricks created

But YOU don't control that bucket!
Databricks manages it!
Scenario 2 — Unity Catalog (New Way)
When you create Unity Catalog Metastore
you assign a DEFAULT STORAGE LOCATION:

Example:
abfss://unity-catalog@yourstorage.dfs.core.windows.net/

Managed table gets stored at:
abfss://unity-catalog@yourstorage.dfs.core.windows.net/
    └── catalog_name/
         └── schema_name/
              └── table_name/

Example:
main/sales/orders/
Storage Priority — Unity Catalog
Unity Catalog checks in this order:

1. Table level storage set?     → Store there
         ↓ No
2. Schema level storage set?    → Store there
         ↓ No
3. Catalog level storage set?   → Store there
         ↓ No
4. Metastore default location   → Store there
How to Check Where Your Table is Stored
-- Check table location
DESCRIBE DETAIL sales.orders;

-- Shows:
-- location: abfss://container@storage/main/sales/orders
-- type: managed

-- Or
DESCRIBE EXTENDED sales.orders;
Key Point to Remember ⚠️
Managed Table:
→ You don't specify location
→ Databricks automatically decides location
→ DROP TABLE = data deleted from that location!

This is exactly why Production teams
prefer External Tables —
because they explicitly control
WHERE data lives!
🎯 One Line Interview Answer
"Managed table location depends on setup — in Hive Metastore it goes to dbfs:/user/hive/warehouse/ which maps to Databricks managed cloud storage. In Unity Catalog it follows priority order — table level, then schema level, then catalog level, then metastore default storage location. Either way Databricks controls the location — and DROP TABLE permanently deletes data from that location — which is why External Tables are preferred in production!"


