🎯 Interview Answer
"Batch processing reads a fixed dataset at scheduled intervals — like running a nightly ETL job that processes entire day's data at once. Simple, cost efficient but data is always delayed.
Structured Streaming processes data continuously as it arrives — treating the incoming data as an infinite growing table. Spark detects new data, processes it in micro batches and writes output immediately with seconds of latency. Key concepts are checkpoint location which ensures fault tolerance and exactly once processing, watermark which handles late arriving data, trigger which controls processing frequency, and output mode which controls how results are written.
In Databricks the recommended pattern is Medallion Architecture — Autoloader streams raw files into Bronze layer, streaming or batch cleans data into Silver, and batch aggregations produce Gold layer for business reporting. Choice between batch and streaming depends on how fresh the data needs to be and the cost tolerance of the project!"


trigger(once=True) vs trigger(availableNow=True) 🎯
Simple One Line Difference
trigger(once=True)        → Process ALL pending data in ONE single batch → Stop
trigger(availableNow=True) → Process ALL pending data in MULTIPLE batches → Stop
trigger(once=True) — Old Way
How it Works
Pending data in source:
File1 → 1GB
File2 → 1GB
File3 → 1GB
File4 → 1GB
Total → 4GB pending

trigger(once=True):
→ Reads ALL 4GB in ONE single batch
→ Processes everything together
→ Stops

ONE massive batch = done!
Code Example
df = spark.readStream \
    .format("cloudFiles") \
    .option("cloudFiles.format", "csv") \
    .option("header", "true") \
    .load("abfss://container@storage/raw/")

df.writeStream \
    .format("delta") \
    .option("checkpointLocation", 
            "abfss://container@storage/checkpoints/") \
    .trigger(once=True) \          # ONE batch all data
    .toTable("main.bronze.orders")
Problem with once=True
Scenario:
→ 100GB pending data
→ trigger(once=True) loads ALL 100GB
   in single batch!

Problems:
❌ Needs huge cluster to handle 100GB at once
❌ If job fails at 99GB → restart from beginning!
   (checkpoint saved only at end of full batch)
❌ Memory issues for large data
❌ Very slow — one massive operation
❌ Deprecated now! Databricks discouraging use
trigger(availableNow=True) — New Way ✅
How it Works
Same pending data:
File1 → 1GB
File2 → 1GB
File3 → 1GB
File4 → 1GB
Total → 4GB pending

trigger(availableNow=True):
→ Batch 1: Process File1 (1GB) → checkpoint saved ✅
→ Batch 2: Process File2 (1GB) → checkpoint saved ✅
→ Batch 3: Process File3 (1GB) → checkpoint saved ✅
→ Batch 4: Process File4 (1GB) → checkpoint saved ✅
→ No more data → STOP!

MULTIPLE small batches = done!
Code Example
df = spark.readStream \
    .format("cloudFiles") \
    .option("cloudFiles.format", "csv") \
    .option("header", "true") \
    .load("abfss://container@storage/raw/")

df.writeStream \
    .format("delta") \
    .option("checkpointLocation",
            "abfss://container@storage/checkpoints/") \
    .trigger(availableNow=True) \   # Multiple batches
    .toTable("main.bronze.orders")
Why availableNow is Better
Same 100GB scenario:
→ Batch 1: 10GB → checkpoint ✅
→ Batch 2: 10GB → checkpoint ✅
→ Batch 3: 10GB → checkpoint ✅
...
→ Batch 10: 10GB → checkpoint ✅
→ Stop!

Benefits:
✅ Smaller cluster needed
✅ Checkpoint saved after EACH batch
✅ Job fails at batch 7?
   → Restart from batch 8!
   → Not from beginning!
✅ Better memory management
✅ More reliable
✅ Databricks recommended approach!
Real World Example — Daily Pipeline
Scenario:
Files land in S3 throughout the day
Every hour you want to process new files only

Schedule: Run every hour using Databricks Jobs

Using trigger(once=True):
8AM  → All files since last run → 1 MASSIVE batch
9AM  → All files since last run → 1 MASSIVE batch
10AM → All files since last run → 1 MASSIVE batch

Problem:
→ If 50 files arrived between 8-9AM
→ All 50 processed in single batch
→ Memory pressure!
→ Failure = restart all 50!

Using trigger(availableNow=True):
8AM  → File1 batch → File2 batch → ... → File50 batch → Stop
9AM  → File51 batch → File52 batch → ... → Stop
10AM → next files → Stop

Benefits:
→ Each file in own batch
→ Failure at File30?
→ Restart from File31!
→ Much safer! ✅
Comparison Table

trigger(once=True)
trigger(availableNow=True)
Batches
Single batch
Multiple batches
Checkpoint
End of job only
After each batch ✅
Failure recovery
Restart everything
Restart from last batch ✅
