Real World Usage Pattern
Bronze Layer (Raw Ingestion):
→ addNewColumns or rescue
→ Capture everything!
→ Zero interruption!

Silver Layer (Cleaned Data):
→ failOnNewColumns or none
→ Schema must be controlled!
→ No surprises allowed!

Gold Layer (Business Ready):
→ none
→ Fixed schema for BI tools!
→ Strict contract!
🎯 Interview Answer
"cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode controls how Autoloader handles schema changes in incoming files.
addNewColumns automatically adds new columns to output table keeping stream running — best for Bronze layer ingestion.
none ignores new columns silently — stream continues but new column data is lost — good for fixed schema environments but risky due to silent data loss.
failOnNewColumns stops stream immediately when new column detected — forces team to review change before resuming — best for governed production pipelines where schema changes need approval.
rescue is most powerful — new columns not added to main schema but ALL unexpected data captured in special _rescued_data JSON column — zero data loss, stream continues, parse rescued data later when needed.
Typical pattern is rescue or addNewColumns for Bronze raw ingestion, failOnNewColumns for Silver controlled processing, and none for Gold fixed schema layer!"

