Core Concepts

1️⃣ Streaming Table vs Materialized View vs View
Three types of DLT datasets:

STREAMING TABLE:
→ Processes new data incrementally
→ Each run processes only new records
→ Good for: Bronze, Silver layers
→ Supports append and CDC

MATERIALIZED VIEW:
→ Recomputes result based on latest data
→ Like a cached query result
→ Good for: Aggregations, Gold layer
→ Always reflects current state

VIEW:
→ Virtual — no data stored
→ Just a logical transformation
→ Used for intermediate steps
→ Not stored physically

Three different read methods in DLT
spark.readStream - Outside DLT (Regular streaming)
dlt.read_stream - Inside DLT (streaming table)
dlt.read - Inside DLT (batch/static read)

2️⃣ How to Define DLT Tables
import dlt
from pyspark.sql.functions import *

# STREAMING TABLE — Bronze layer
@dlt.table(
    name = "bronze_orders",
    comment = "Raw orders from cloud storage"
)
def bronze_orders():
    return (
        spark.readStream
            .format("cloudFiles")
            .option("cloudFiles.format", "json")
            .load("abfss://container@storage/raw/orders/")
    )


# STREAMING TABLE — Silver layer
@dlt.table(
    name = "silver_orders",
    comment = "Cleaned and validated orders"
)
def silver_orders():
    return (
        dlt.read_stream("bronze_orders")  # Read from bronze!
            .filter(col("amount") > 0)
            .withColumn("order_date",
                       col("order_date").cast("date"))
            .dropDuplicates(["order_id"])
    )


# MATERIALIZED VIEW — Gold layer
materiakized views are always correct because they automatically recompute when source data chnages - but their downside is higher latency
because they might process all input data to ensure queries return correct results
@dlt.table(
    name = "gold_revenue_summary",
    comment = "Daily revenue by region"
)
def gold_revenue_summary():
    return (
        dlt.read("silver_orders")  # Read from silver!
            .groupBy("region", "order_date")
            .agg(
                sum("amount").alias("total_revenue"),
                count("*").alias("order_count")
            )
    ).


3️⃣ Data Quality — Expectations ⭐ (total 6 expectation types in total)
This is the BIGGEST feature of DLT!
# Define rules — what GOOD data looks like!

@dlt.table(name = "silver_orders")
@dlt.expect("valid_amount", "amount > 0")
@dlt.expect("valid_order_id", "order_id IS NOT NULL")
@dlt.expect("valid_date", "order_date IS NOT NULL")
def silver_orders():
    return dlt.read_stream("bronze_orders")
Three Types of Expectations
# 1. expect — WARN but keep bad records
@dlt.expect("valid_amount", "amount > 0")
# Bad records kept in table
# Warning shown in pipeline UI
# Good for: monitoring data quality

# 2. expect_or_drop — DROP bad records
@dlt.expect_or_drop("valid_amount", "amount > 0")
# Bad records REMOVED from output
# Good for: preventing bad data downstream

# 3. expect_or_fail — FAIL pipeline
@dlt.expect_or_fail("valid_amount", "amount > 0")
# Bad records cause PIPELINE FAILURE!
# Good for: critical data that must be perfect
Real Data Quality Example
@dlt.table(
    name = "silver_orders",
    comment = "Validated orders"
)
@dlt.expect("amount_positive",
            "amount > 0")
@dlt.expect_or_drop("order_id_not_null",
                    "order_id IS NOT NULL")
@dlt.expect_or_fail("customer_id_valid",
                    "customer_id IS NOT NULL AND customer_id > 0")
def silver_orders():
    return (
        dlt.read_stream("bronze_orders")
            .select(
                "order_id",
                "customer_id",
                "amount",
                "region",
                "order_date"
            )
    )

# Result:
# amount <= 0        → Warning, kept in table
# order_id null      → Record DROPPED
# customer_id null   → Pipeline FAILS!

Group Expectation Decorators(3) 
expect_all 
These decorators accept a python dictionary as argument where key is expectation name and value is a constraint
- allowing you to group multiple expectations and apply collective actions.
expect_all
expect_all_or_drop - drop if any rule fails
expect_all_or_fail - fail if any rule fails

when to use which?
single rules (expect, expect_or_drop, expect_or_fail)
few rules per table
different actions for different rules
supports in sql or python

Group rules (expect_all,expect_all_or_drop,expect_all_or_fail)
Many rules per tables
same action for all rules
reuse rules across multiple tables
cleaner code
python only



